  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The sour gold-pored bolete (Aureoboletus auriporus) is a medium-sized fungus recognized by its golden-yellow pores and distinctive sour taste. Despite its appealing appearance, it is inedible and can cause gastrointestinal distress if consumed.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Golden-yellow pores",
      "description": "The underside features bright golden-yellow pores that may bruise slightly when handled",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Sour taste",
      "description": "A small taste test (spit out immediately) reveals a distinctly sour flavor, which is the origin of its common name",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown cap and stem",
      "description": "The cap is typically brown to grayish-brown, and the stem is often yellowish or olive-tinged",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This bolete is found in New England forests, typically under hardwoods and mixed woodlands during summer and fall months.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The sour taste that makes this bolete inedible is actually a natural defense mechanism that helps the fungus discourage animals from consuming its fruiting body.",
